{"id":"n-20261013-q41kd7","category":"travel","display_title":"Exploring Thuringia By Bus","display_title_he":"טיול בתורינגיה באוטובוס","short_summary":"A four-day car-free journey through Germany's Thuringia region reveals medieval cities, historic towns, and vast national parks accessible entirely by public transport.","english_level":"C1","words":459,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/13/thuringia-germanys-green-heart-by-bus","title":"‘A winning combination of culture and nature’: exploring Germany’s green heart by bus","published_at":"2026-10-13T06:00:17.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-13T06:00:17.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-13T07:57:09.576Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Nestled in the central part of Germany, the region of Thuringia offers a compelling mixture of rich intellectual heritage, dense forests, and historic architecture that can be explored entirely without a car. A four-day itinerary through this green heart of the country begins in Erfurt, the regional capital, where the bustling morning market at Domplatz unfolds beneath towering medieval structures. Among these architectural engineering wonders is Erfurt Cathedral, which houses the world's largest medieval free-swinging bell. Cast in 1497, the massive bell weighs twelve tonnes and anchors a skyline punctuated by ancient steeples and church towers.","Erfurt retains an atmospheric old town that has changed remarkably little over the centuries. A brief walk from the cathedral leads visitors to the Krämerbrücke, a notable 14th-century bridge spanning one hundred and twenty metres and lined with colorful half-timbered houses. Historically positioned at the intersection of major trade routes, the city accumulated wealth from waid, a local plant processed into blue dye. Nearby stands the Petersberg Citadel, an expansive European fortress featuring a complex labyrinth of underground tunnels, while the Augustinian monastery marks the location where Martin Luther once lived as a monk and pursued his studies. Culinary traditions can be experienced at the historic Wirtshaus Christoffel, serving house-brewed beer and beef roulade with Thuringian dumplings.","Continuing the journey via a short bus ride brings travelers to Weimar, a city renowned as the birthplace of the artistic Bauhaus movement and the site where Germany adopted its initial democratic constitution. The local Bauhaus museum exhibits various ceramics, textiles, and metalwork, complementing the surrounding historic architecture and the former art school buildings that now constitute the Bauhaus-Universität.","For a shift from cultural exploration to relaxation, visitors can take another bus to Bad Langensalza, a thermal spa town featuring ten parks and distinct themed gardens. Following the discovery of local sulphur springs in 1811, the town developed facilities such as the Friederiken Therme, which supplies natural sulphur, brine, and drinking mineral waters alongside various pools and treatments. The surrounding area also preserves the Klagetor gate tower from approximately 1365, which transitions into a traditional Japanese garden complete with koi ponds and clipped azaleas.","True to its nickname as Germany's green heart, Thuringia provides grand-scale natural landscapes at Hainich national park, reachable within minutes by bus or bicycle. Spanning seventy-five hundred hectares, Hainich comprises the country's largest unbroken deciduous woodland area, featuring extensive beech forests left completely untouched by human interference for decades. Visitors can explore the region via the Baumkronenpfad, a wooden treetop walkway leading to a forty-four-metre observation tower that places ancient oak canopies directly at eye level. This balanced itinerary concludes in Erfurt with a final symphony concert, demonstrating how culture and pristine nature intersect harmoniously across Thuringia through convenient public transport connections."],"facts":[{"fact":"A traveler explored Thuringia, Germany over four days using public transport and buses.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Erfurt is the capital of Thuringia, featuring Domplatz and the 14th-century Krämerbrücke bridge.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Erfurt Cathedral houses the Gloriosa, the world's largest medieval free-swinging bell, cast in 1497 and weighing 12 tonnes.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Petersberg Citadel in Erfurt is a well-preserved fortress with underground tunnels.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Weimar is the birthplace of the Bauhaus movement and the location of Germany's first democratic constitution adoption.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Bad Langensalza is a spa town featuring 10 parks, themed gardens, and thermal baths like Friederiken Therme utilizing sulphur springs.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Hainich national park spans 7,500 hectares of deciduous woodland and features a tree canopy walkway called Baumkronenpfad.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Nestled","lemma":"nestle","pos":"verb","he":"שרוי בנעימות, מונח בחמימות","name":"false"},{"text":"central","lemma":"central","pos":"adjective","he":"מרכזי","name":false},{"text":"part","lemma":"part","pos":"noun","he":"חלק","name":false},{"text":"Germany","lemma":"Germany","pos":"proper noun","he":"גרמניה","name":"true"},{"text":"region","lemma":"region","pos":"noun","he":"אזור","name":false},{"text":"Thuringia","lemma":"Thuringia","pos":"proper noun","he":"תורינגיה","name":"true"},{"text":"offers","lemma":"offer","pos":"verb","he":"מציע","name":false},{"text":"compelling","lemma":"compelling","pos":"adjective","he":"מרתק, משכנע","name":false},{"text":"mixture","lemma":"mixture","pos":"noun","he":"תערובת","name":false},{"text":"rich","lemma":"rich","pos":"adjective","he":"עשיר","name":false},{"text":"intellectual","lemma":"intellectual","pos":"adjective","he":"אינטלקטואלי"